1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,135
What would you do if someone offered

2
00:00:03,135 --> 00:00:05,056
you a barrel of oil,

3
00:00:05,056 --> 00:00:07,327
and paid you to take it?

4
00:00:07,583 --> 00:00:09,001
On April twentieth,

5
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:10,179
twenty twenty,

6
00:00:10,179 --> 00:00:12,943
that is exactly what happened.

7
00:00:13,173 --> 00:00:15,601
The price of American crude oil for

8
00:00:15,601 --> 00:00:18,490
delivery in May fell below zero,

9
00:00:18,490 --> 00:00:20,432
for the first time since that contract

10
00:00:20,432 --> 00:00:23,703
began trading in nineteen eighty-three.

11
00:00:24,033 --> 00:00:25,605
At the end of the day it

12
00:00:25,605 --> 00:00:28,301
settled at minus thirty-seven dollars and

13
00:00:28,301 --> 00:00:30,473
sixty-three cents a barrel.

14
00:00:30,663 --> 00:00:32,963
People who owned oil were paying others

15
00:00:32,963 --> 00:00:34,943
to take it off their hands.

16
00:00:35,113 --> 00:00:36,593
To understand how,

17
00:00:36,593 --> 00:00:38,851
you have to understand that an oil

18
00:00:38,851 --> 00:00:40,833
price is really a promise,

19
00:00:40,833 --> 00:00:42,841
and on that day the promise came

20
00:00:42,841 --> 00:00:43,691
due.

21
00:00:54,350 --> 00:00:56,850
What happens to oil when the world

22
00:00:56,850 --> 00:00:58,388
stops moving?

23
00:01:03,350 --> 00:01:05,501
In the spring of twenty twenty,

24
00:01:05,501 --> 00:01:07,677
the pandemic shut down much of the

25
00:01:07,677 --> 00:01:08,740
planet.

26
00:01:08,740 --> 00:01:10,800
Planes stayed on the ground.

27
00:01:10,800 --> 00:01:12,440
Roads emptied.

28
00:01:12,440 --> 00:01:14,510
Factories went quiet.

29
00:01:14,710 --> 00:01:17,430
Analysts cited by the US Congressional

30
00:01:17,430 --> 00:01:19,924
Research Service estimated that global

31
00:01:19,924 --> 00:01:21,964
demand for crude oil fell by about

32
00:01:21,964 --> 00:01:23,981
thirty million barrels a day,

33
00:01:23,981 --> 00:01:26,701
around thirty percent of everything the

34
00:01:26,701 --> 00:01:28,435
world normally burns.

35
00:01:28,683 --> 00:01:31,463
The big producers tried to respond.

36
00:01:31,463 --> 00:01:32,552
On April twelfth,

37
00:01:32,552 --> 00:01:34,553
the OPEC Plus group agreed to cut

38
00:01:34,553 --> 00:01:36,998
output by nine point seven million barrels

39
00:01:36,998 --> 00:01:37,643
a day,

40
00:01:37,643 --> 00:01:39,866
the largest single production cut in

41
00:01:39,866 --> 00:01:41,133
history.

42
00:01:41,203 --> 00:01:43,453
But it would only start in May.

43
00:01:43,563 --> 00:01:45,428
And even a record cut was much

44
00:01:45,428 --> 00:01:47,892
smaller than the hole in demand.

45
00:01:48,150 --> 00:01:50,460
Let me draw the problem.

46
00:01:50,920 --> 00:01:52,503
Here is the oil coming out of

47
00:01:52,503 --> 00:01:54,290
the ground every day.

48
00:01:54,580 --> 00:01:56,056
Here is the oil the world was

49
00:01:56,056 --> 00:01:57,710
actually using.

50
00:01:58,060 --> 00:02:01,630
The gap between them did not disappear.

51
00:02:01,870 --> 00:02:04,251
Every barrel nobody wanted had to go

52
00:02:04,251 --> 00:02:05,400
somewhere.

53
00:02:05,520 --> 00:02:06,596
Into a tank,

54
00:02:06,596 --> 00:02:07,671
a pipeline,

55
00:02:07,671 --> 00:02:08,760
or a ship.

56
00:02:08,760 --> 00:02:10,621
And the space in those tanks was

57
00:02:10,621 --> 00:02:12,019
running out.

58
00:02:12,283 --> 00:02:15,161
To see why that mattered so much,

59
00:02:15,161 --> 00:02:17,195
you need to know how oil is

60
00:02:17,195 --> 00:02:18,958
really traded.

61
00:02:24,617 --> 00:02:26,937
When you buy oil on the market,

62
00:02:26,937 --> 00:02:29,361
what do you actually own?

63
00:02:33,617 --> 00:02:36,104
Most oil is traded through futures

64
00:02:36,104 --> 00:02:37,247
contracts.

65
00:02:37,247 --> 00:02:39,653
A futures contract is a promise to

66
00:02:39,653 --> 00:02:41,404
buy or sell barrels of oil at

67
00:02:41,404 --> 00:02:42,257
a set price,

68
00:02:42,257 --> 00:02:44,607
on a set date in the future.

69
00:02:44,937 --> 00:02:47,721
The best known American contract is called

70
00:02:47,721 --> 00:02:49,017
W T I.

71
00:02:49,097 --> 00:02:50,906
Each month has its own contract,

72
00:02:50,906 --> 00:02:52,738
and each one ends on a fixed

73
00:02:52,738 --> 00:02:53,567
day.

74
00:02:53,727 --> 00:02:56,580
The May twenty twenty contract expired on

75
00:02:56,580 --> 00:02:58,369
April twenty-first.

76
00:02:58,617 --> 00:03:01,187
And here is the key.

77
00:03:01,277 --> 00:03:03,827
These contracts are for real oil.

78
00:03:03,917 --> 00:03:06,904
According to the US Energy Information

79
00:03:06,904 --> 00:03:08,259
Administration,

80
00:03:08,259 --> 00:03:10,786
anyone still holding one when it expires

81
00:03:10,786 --> 00:03:13,543
must make or take physical delivery of

82
00:03:13,543 --> 00:03:14,898
crude oil in Cushing,

83
00:03:14,898 --> 00:03:16,024
Oklahoma,

84
00:03:16,024 --> 00:03:17,632
a small town that is home to

85
00:03:17,632 --> 00:03:19,840
a major oil storage hub.

86
00:03:20,083 --> 00:03:22,693
Let me draw what that means.

87
00:03:22,803 --> 00:03:24,556
Here is a trader who bought a

88
00:03:24,556 --> 00:03:25,813
May contract.

89
00:03:25,813 --> 00:03:27,730
Before the expiry date,

90
00:03:27,730 --> 00:03:29,543
they have two choices.

91
00:03:29,613 --> 00:03:31,863
Sell the contract to someone else.

92
00:03:31,863 --> 00:03:32,737
Or keep it,

93
00:03:32,737 --> 00:03:34,753
and receive real barrels of oil in

94
00:03:34,753 --> 00:03:35,851
Oklahoma,

95
00:03:35,851 --> 00:03:38,243
and find somewhere to put them.

96
00:03:38,423 --> 00:03:40,790
Most financial traders have no tanks and

97
00:03:40,790 --> 00:03:42,493
no pipelines.

98
00:03:42,493 --> 00:03:43,470
So for them,

99
00:03:43,470 --> 00:03:45,713
there is only one real choice.

100
00:03:45,713 --> 00:03:47,996
Sell before the deadline.

101
00:03:48,250 --> 00:03:50,880
Most people did leave in time.

102
00:03:50,990 --> 00:03:53,269
According to the US regulator,

103
00:03:53,269 --> 00:03:56,175
the Commodity Futures Trading Commission,

104
00:03:56,175 --> 00:03:58,265
the number of open May contracts fell

105
00:03:58,265 --> 00:04:00,356
from about six hundred and thirty-five

106
00:04:00,356 --> 00:04:02,237
thousand at the start of April to

107
00:04:02,237 --> 00:04:04,328
about one hundred and nine thousand on

108
00:04:04,328 --> 00:04:06,600
the morning of April twentieth.

109
00:04:06,800 --> 00:04:08,767
But that still left a lot of

110
00:04:08,767 --> 00:04:09,582
contracts,

111
00:04:09,582 --> 00:04:11,587
with one day to go.

112
00:04:11,850 --> 00:04:13,647
And to take delivery,

113
00:04:13,647 --> 00:04:15,760
you need one thing above all.

114
00:04:15,760 --> 00:04:18,100
Somewhere to put the oil.

115
00:04:23,750 --> 00:04:28,126
What happens when the tanks are full?

116
00:04:32,750 --> 00:04:35,229
The storage tanks at Cushing can hold

117
00:04:35,229 --> 00:04:38,535
about seventy-six million barrels of crude

118
00:04:38,535 --> 00:04:39,410
oil.

119
00:04:39,630 --> 00:04:40,575
In mid-March,

120
00:04:40,575 --> 00:04:42,630
they were about half full.

121
00:04:42,790 --> 00:04:44,615
By April seventeenth,

122
00:04:44,615 --> 00:04:47,330
they held sixty million barrels.

123
00:04:47,410 --> 00:04:49,938
They were seventy-six percent full,

124
00:04:49,938 --> 00:04:51,560
and filling fast.

125
00:04:51,960 --> 00:04:54,310
The three weeks to April seventeenth

126
00:04:54,310 --> 00:04:56,922
brought the three largest weekly increases

127
00:04:56,922 --> 00:04:58,827
ever recorded.

128
00:04:59,083 --> 00:05:01,937
And seventy-six percent full did not mean

129
00:05:01,937 --> 00:05:04,093
twenty-four percent free.

130
00:05:04,523 --> 00:05:07,241
As the Energy Information Administration

131
00:05:07,241 --> 00:05:07,804
noted,

132
00:05:07,804 --> 00:05:09,745
some of the empty space had probably

133
00:05:09,745 --> 00:05:12,269
already been leased or promised to someone

134
00:05:12,269 --> 00:05:13,063
else.

135
00:05:13,383 --> 00:05:15,263
For a trader looking for a tank

136
00:05:15,263 --> 00:05:16,495
at the last minute,

137
00:05:16,495 --> 00:05:18,557
there was almost nothing left.

138
00:05:18,817 --> 00:05:21,178
Oil companies started looking for storage

139
00:05:21,178 --> 00:05:22,874
anywhere they could find it,

140
00:05:22,874 --> 00:05:24,547
including at sea.

141
00:05:24,717 --> 00:05:26,716
In the first three weeks of April,

142
00:05:26,716 --> 00:05:28,737
the oil stored on tankers floating

143
00:05:28,737 --> 00:05:31,207
offshore rose by almost sixty-nine million

144
00:05:31,207 --> 00:05:31,858
barrels,

145
00:05:31,858 --> 00:05:34,329
to about one hundred and twenty-seven

146
00:05:34,329 --> 00:05:35,827
million barrels.

147
00:05:35,917 --> 00:05:38,596
Ships became floating warehouses.

148
00:05:38,850 --> 00:05:41,343
And the tanks kept filling after the

149
00:05:41,343 --> 00:05:42,220
crash.

150
00:05:42,420 --> 00:05:43,697
A week later,

151
00:05:43,697 --> 00:05:45,495
on April twenty-fourth,

152
00:05:45,495 --> 00:05:48,180
Cushing was eighty-one percent full.

153
00:05:48,420 --> 00:05:49,494
By the start of May,

154
00:05:49,494 --> 00:05:51,680
according to the Energy Information

155
00:05:51,680 --> 00:05:52,754
Administration,

156
00:05:52,754 --> 00:05:54,757
it had reached eighty-three percent of its

157
00:05:54,757 --> 00:05:56,450
working capacity.

158
00:05:56,770 --> 00:06:00,210
The hub was never completely full.

159
00:06:00,260 --> 00:06:02,751
But for anyone holding a May contract

160
00:06:02,751 --> 00:06:04,718
with nowhere to send the oil,

161
00:06:04,718 --> 00:06:06,812
it did not need to be.

162
00:06:07,050 --> 00:06:09,218
Let me draw what storage does to

163
00:06:09,218 --> 00:06:10,290
a price.

164
00:06:10,290 --> 00:06:11,105
Normally,

165
00:06:11,105 --> 00:06:13,196
a barrel today and a barrel next

166
00:06:13,196 --> 00:06:15,050
month cost about the same.

167
00:06:15,130 --> 00:06:16,639
But when storage runs out,

168
00:06:16,639 --> 00:06:19,263
oil for delivery right now becomes a

169
00:06:19,263 --> 00:06:20,220
burden.

170
00:06:20,220 --> 00:06:21,527
You have to pay someone to keep

171
00:06:21,527 --> 00:06:22,290
it.

172
00:06:22,300 --> 00:06:23,704
So the near month falls,

173
00:06:23,704 --> 00:06:25,970
and the later months hold up.

174
00:06:26,120 --> 00:06:27,737
The gap between them is the price

175
00:06:27,737 --> 00:06:28,943
of a tank.

176
00:06:29,183 --> 00:06:30,213
On Friday,

177
00:06:30,213 --> 00:06:31,772
April seventeenth,

178
00:06:31,772 --> 00:06:34,150
the May contract closed at eighteen

179
00:06:34,150 --> 00:06:36,863
dollars and twenty-seven cents.

180
00:06:36,963 --> 00:06:39,059
Then came Monday.

181
00:06:44,717 --> 00:06:47,299
How can the price of something real

182
00:06:47,299 --> 00:06:49,333
fall below nothing?

183
00:06:53,717 --> 00:06:54,809
On Monday,

184
00:06:54,809 --> 00:06:56,182
April twentieth,

185
00:06:56,182 --> 00:06:59,516
the last full trading day before expiry,

186
00:06:59,516 --> 00:07:02,317
the May contract started falling and kept

187
00:07:02,317 --> 00:07:03,477
falling.

188
00:07:03,737 --> 00:07:06,394
Holders who could not take delivery needed

189
00:07:06,394 --> 00:07:07,437
to sell.

190
00:07:07,687 --> 00:07:09,212
But there were very few buyers with

191
00:07:09,212 --> 00:07:11,147
somewhere to put the oil.

192
00:07:11,447 --> 00:07:13,759
By the early afternoon in New York,

193
00:07:13,759 --> 00:07:16,055
the price was approaching zero.

194
00:07:16,317 --> 00:07:18,647
Then it went through the floor.

195
00:07:18,647 --> 00:07:20,836
According to the regulator,

196
00:07:20,836 --> 00:07:22,361
in twenty minutes,

197
00:07:22,361 --> 00:07:24,350
between eight minutes past two and

198
00:07:24,350 --> 00:07:26,097
twenty-eight minutes past two,

199
00:07:26,097 --> 00:07:28,086
the price fell from zero to minus

200
00:07:28,086 --> 00:07:30,787
thirty-nine dollars and fifty-five cents.

201
00:07:30,787 --> 00:07:33,403
A minute later it hit minus forty

202
00:07:33,403 --> 00:07:35,282
dollars and thirty-two cents,

203
00:07:35,282 --> 00:07:37,660
the lowest price in the history of

204
00:07:37,660 --> 00:07:38,974
the contract.

205
00:07:39,217 --> 00:07:40,239
At the close,

206
00:07:40,239 --> 00:07:42,596
the May contract settled at minus

207
00:07:42,596 --> 00:07:45,215
thirty-seven dollars and sixty-three

208
00:07:45,215 --> 00:07:46,077
cents.

209
00:07:46,387 --> 00:07:48,365
From the start of trading to the

210
00:07:48,365 --> 00:07:49,082
bottom,

211
00:07:49,082 --> 00:07:51,308
it had lost fifty-eight dollars and five

212
00:07:51,308 --> 00:07:52,897
cents a barrel.

213
00:07:53,177 --> 00:07:54,419
Some sellers were,

214
00:07:54,419 --> 00:07:55,408
in effect,

215
00:07:55,408 --> 00:07:57,943
paying a counterparty to let them out

216
00:07:57,943 --> 00:07:59,557
of their contracts.

217
00:07:59,817 --> 00:08:02,959
The strangest number was the gap between

218
00:08:02,959 --> 00:08:04,187
two months.

219
00:08:04,197 --> 00:08:05,136
On Friday,

220
00:08:05,136 --> 00:08:07,543
May oil had been about seven dollars

221
00:08:07,543 --> 00:08:09,347
cheaper than June oil.

222
00:08:09,547 --> 00:08:10,918
By Monday's close,

223
00:08:10,918 --> 00:08:13,477
the gap was fifty-eight dollars.

224
00:08:13,527 --> 00:08:14,650
The June contract,

225
00:08:14,650 --> 00:08:16,942
for the same oil delivered just one

226
00:08:16,942 --> 00:08:17,836
month later,

227
00:08:17,836 --> 00:08:20,358
closed at twenty dollars and forty-three

228
00:08:20,358 --> 00:08:21,187
cents.

229
00:08:21,307 --> 00:08:23,937
Nothing had happened to the oil itself.

230
00:08:24,007 --> 00:08:25,798
Only to the date.

231
00:08:26,050 --> 00:08:28,270
Let me draw that Monday.

232
00:08:28,310 --> 00:08:30,967
Here is the May contract on Friday,

233
00:08:30,967 --> 00:08:32,640
eighteen dollars.

234
00:08:32,770 --> 00:08:35,283
Here is the June contract on Monday,

235
00:08:35,283 --> 00:08:37,660
still around twenty dollars.

236
00:08:37,710 --> 00:08:39,658
And here is the May contract at

237
00:08:39,658 --> 00:08:40,365
the close,

238
00:08:40,365 --> 00:08:43,400
almost thirty-eight dollars below zero.

239
00:08:43,570 --> 00:08:44,658
The same oil,

240
00:08:44,658 --> 00:08:46,304
the same pipeline,

241
00:08:46,304 --> 00:08:48,020
one month apart.

242
00:08:48,130 --> 00:08:51,170
All the other months stayed above zero.

243
00:08:51,400 --> 00:08:53,708
Only the one that was about to

244
00:08:53,708 --> 00:08:55,590
arrive collapsed.

245
00:08:55,850 --> 00:08:56,970
The next morning,

246
00:08:56,970 --> 00:08:59,256
the world woke up to headlines about

247
00:08:59,256 --> 00:09:00,770
negative oil.

248
00:09:00,910 --> 00:09:03,392
But the story was not over.

249
00:09:09,050 --> 00:09:11,578
Who was left holding the oil?

250
00:09:18,050 --> 00:09:19,997
On April twenty-first,

251
00:09:19,997 --> 00:09:21,097
its last day,

252
00:09:21,097 --> 00:09:23,919
the May contract settled at ten dollars

253
00:09:23,919 --> 00:09:25,019
and one cent,

254
00:09:25,019 --> 00:09:27,030
positive again.

255
00:09:27,170 --> 00:09:29,072
But the panic spread to the next

256
00:09:29,072 --> 00:09:29,910
month.

257
00:09:30,130 --> 00:09:32,484
The June contract fell by forty-three

258
00:09:32,484 --> 00:09:33,765
percent that day,

259
00:09:33,765 --> 00:09:37,504
to eleven dollars and fifty-seven cents.

260
00:09:37,750 --> 00:09:39,910
The regulator later looked at who was

261
00:09:39,910 --> 00:09:40,870
trading.

262
00:09:40,980 --> 00:09:42,506
On April twentieth,

263
00:09:42,506 --> 00:09:44,833
out of four hundred and forty-eight large

264
00:09:44,833 --> 00:09:46,358
traders it tracked,

265
00:09:46,358 --> 00:09:48,944
one hundred and seventy-three still held

266
00:09:48,944 --> 00:09:50,320
May contracts.

267
00:09:50,470 --> 00:09:52,917
Its interim report described the

268
00:09:52,917 --> 00:09:54,606
conditions in detail,

269
00:09:54,606 --> 00:09:56,564
but did not name a single cause

270
00:09:56,564 --> 00:09:57,898
for the crash.

271
00:09:58,150 --> 00:10:01,320
The exchange had seen the risk coming.

272
00:10:01,520 --> 00:10:02,892
In early April,

273
00:10:02,892 --> 00:10:05,218
CME Group sent notices to its members

274
00:10:05,218 --> 00:10:08,683
about the possibility of negative prices,

275
00:10:08,683 --> 00:10:11,241
and opened a testing system so that

276
00:10:11,241 --> 00:10:12,869
trading firms could check that their

277
00:10:12,869 --> 00:10:16,260
computers could handle a price below zero.

278
00:10:16,820 --> 00:10:18,979
In almost forty years of trading,

279
00:10:18,979 --> 00:10:21,542
that had never happened before.

280
00:10:21,783 --> 00:10:24,436
As lockdowns eased and demand slowly

281
00:10:24,436 --> 00:10:25,471
returned,

282
00:10:25,471 --> 00:10:27,663
the price recovered.

283
00:10:27,723 --> 00:10:28,819
By July first,

284
00:10:28,819 --> 00:10:31,278
a barrel of American oil was back

285
00:10:31,278 --> 00:10:33,443
at about forty dollars.

286
00:10:33,643 --> 00:10:37,044
Prices below zero had not even lasted

287
00:10:37,044 --> 00:10:38,778
two trading days.

288
00:10:39,017 --> 00:10:41,375
So what does negative oil teach about

289
00:10:41,375 --> 00:10:42,447
prices?

290
00:10:42,457 --> 00:10:43,957
That a price is not just about

291
00:10:43,957 --> 00:10:46,057
how useful something is.

292
00:10:46,157 --> 00:10:48,245
It is also about where it is,

293
00:10:48,245 --> 00:10:49,490
when it arrives,

294
00:10:49,490 --> 00:10:51,777
and whether you have room for it.

295
00:10:51,977 --> 00:10:53,569
On April twentieth,

296
00:10:53,569 --> 00:10:56,057
the oil had not lost its value.

297
00:10:56,127 --> 00:10:57,782
The world had simply run out of

298
00:10:57,782 --> 00:10:59,417
places to put it.

299
00:10:59,687 --> 00:11:01,381
And a contract that looked like a

300
00:11:01,381 --> 00:11:03,074
piece of paper turned out to be

301
00:11:03,074 --> 00:11:05,520
a promise to take delivery of something

302
00:11:05,520 --> 00:11:06,685
very real.

303
00:11:06,950 --> 00:11:09,103
Next time on The Hidden Economics,

304
00:11:09,103 --> 00:11:11,670
we follow the money behind another hidden

305
00:11:11,670 --> 00:11:13,850
link of the world economy.

306
00:11:14,110 --> 00:11:16,538
This is The Hidden Economics.
